# thingsync — one-way mirror from Things 3 to Apple Reminders

## Context

Things 3 holds the authoritative task list, but Apple Reminders is what's reachable
from Siri, Apple Watch, CarPlay and shared lists. There is no Things Cloud API, so any
integration has to work locally on the Mac: read the Things SQLite database, write via
EventKit.

The goal is a **one-way mirror** — Things is the source of truth, Reminders is a
reflection of it. All open to-dos are mirrored. It runs as a CLI, invoked manually.
Edits made in Reminders are not read back and will be overwritten on the next run.

The project directory `/Users/jsh/Projects/thingsync` is currently empty; this is
greenfield, including `git init`.

## Decisions taken

| Decision | Choice |
|---|---|
| Direction | One-way, Things → Reminders. Reminders is disposable output. |
| Scope | All open (incomplete, non-trashed) to-dos. |
| Trigger | Manual CLI. No launchd agent yet — leave the door open for one. |
| Stack | Python 3.12 via `uv`/`mise`; `things.py` to read, PyObjC EventKit to write. |

## Architecture

```
thingsync/
  pyproject.toml            # uv-managed, entry point `thingsync`
  .mise.toml                # pins python + uv
  src/thingsync/
    cli.py                  # argparse: sync, doctor; --dry-run, --list, --on-done
    things_source.py        # adapter over things.py -> list[ThingsTodo]
    reminders_sink.py       # adapter over EventKit -> create/update/complete/lookup
    model.py                # ThingsTodo, ReminderPayload, SyncAction dataclasses
    mapping.py              # ThingsTodo -> ReminderPayload field mapping (pure)
    planner.py              # (todos, state) -> [SyncAction]  (pure, the core)
    state.py                # load/save JSON state store
  tests/
```

The two adapters are the only macOS-touching code and sit behind narrow protocols
(`TodoSource`, `ReminderSink`). `planner.py` and `mapping.py` are pure and carry the
test weight; adapters are faked in unit tests.

### State store

`~/.local/state/thingsync/state.json`, one entry per mirrored to-do:

```json
{ "version": 1,
  "items": { "<things-uuid>": { "reminder_id": "<calendarItemIdentifier>",
                                "hash": "<sha256 of payload>",
                                "list": "Things" } } }
```

The hash makes re-runs cheap and idempotent: unchanged to-dos are skipped entirely.

**Safety rule, non-negotiable:** thingsync only ever touches reminders whose
identifier appears in its own state file. Hand-made reminders are never modified or
deleted, even in the target list.

### Field mapping (`mapping.py`)

| Things | Reminders |
|---|---|
| `title` | `title` |
| `notes` | `notes` body |
| project / area | breadcrumb line prepended to notes (`Area › Project`) |
| `tags` | appended to notes as `#tag` text — EventKit does not expose Reminders tags |
| `checklist` | appended to notes as `☐ item` lines — see limitations |
| `deadline` | `dueDateComponents`, date-only (all-day) |
| `start_date` (When) | `startDateComponents`, date-only, **no alarm** — avoids a notification storm on first run |
| Inbox / Anytime / Someday | not mapped; everything lands in one list |

### Sync algorithm (`planner.py`)

For each open Things to-do:
- not in state → **Create**
- in state, hash changed → **Update** (look up by identifier; if the reminder is gone,
  the user deleted it → recreate, since Things is the source of truth)
- in state, hash unchanged → no-op

For each state entry whose to-do is no longer open in Things (completed, cancelled,
trashed, or vanished):
- **Complete** the reminder and drop the mapping (default), or **Delete** it with
  `--on-done delete`.

`--dry-run` prints the action list and writes nothing. This is how the first run
should always be done.

## Permissions — the fiddly part

Two separate macOS grants are needed, both attributed to the *host* process (your
terminal), not to the script:

1. **Full Disk Access** for the terminal — without it
   `~/Library/Group Containers/JLMPQHK86H.com.culturedcode.ThingsMac/` is unreadable.
   Confirmed in this session: the path returns `Operation not permitted` today.
2. **Reminders access** — EventKit prompts on first use.
   macOS 14+ requires `requestFullAccessToRemindersWithCompletion:`; the older
   `requestAccessToEntityType:completion:` is deprecated on this machine (macOS 26.6).

`thingsync doctor` checks both, prints exactly which System Settings pane to open, and
exits non-zero if either is missing. Build this early — it saves debugging every
confusing failure later as if it were a code bug.

EventKit note: prefer `calendarItemWithIdentifier:` (synchronous) for updates, since
identifiers come from the state file. Only the access request and any full-list fetch
need the async completion-handler bridge.

## Increments (TDD, one commit each)

1. `git init`; uv/mise scaffold; `pytest` green with one trivial test.
2. `model.py` + `mapping.py`: to-do → payload, incl. notes composition. Pure tests.
3. `state.py`: load/save/round-trip, missing-file and version handling.
4. `planner.py`: create / update / skip / complete decisions against a fake state.
   This is the increment that deserves the most test cases.
5. `things_source.py`: real `things.py` read → `ThingsTodo`. Test against a small
   fixture database rather than the live one.
6. `reminders_sink.py`: EventKit create/update/complete/lookup.
7. `cli.py`: `sync --dry-run`, then real `sync`; `doctor`.
8. `README.md`: permissions setup, usage, the "Reminders is disposable" contract.

## Known limitations (state these in the README)

- **Checklists** flatten into notes text. `EKReminder.parentReminder` exists but is
  undocumented and best-effort; not worth the risk for a v1.
- **Tags** become notes text — EventKit has no public tag API.
- **Repeating to-dos** mirror as the single next instance, with no recurrence rule.
- **Reminders-side edits are lost** on the next run. By design.
- Things must have been launched at least once on this Mac for the database to exist.

## Verification

```sh
uv run pytest                          # unit suite
uv run thingsync doctor                # both permissions green
uv run thingsync sync --dry-run        # inspect the action list, write nothing
uv run thingsync sync --list Scratch   # real run into a throwaway list
```

Then, by hand:
1. Open Reminders — the Scratch list matches the open Things to-dos, with correct
   due dates and notes breadcrumbs.
2. Re-run `sync --list Scratch` — output shows all skips, no duplicates appear.
   This is the idempotence check, and the one most likely to catch a real bug.
3. Complete a to-do in Things, re-run — the matching reminder is completed.
4. Edit a to-do's title in Things, re-run — the reminder is updated in place, not
   duplicated.
5. Delete a mirrored reminder by hand, re-run — it is recreated.
6. Create an unrelated reminder by hand in the same list, re-run — it is untouched.
